WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:17.597
<v Jamie>Restaurant Brands just crushed earnings and raised full-year guidance on the back of Burger King's U.S. turnaround—a chain that was basically the punchline six months ago is now the one making money.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/06/restaurant-brands-international-q2-2026-earnings.html

00:00:17.597 --> 00:00:41.067
<v Alex>So Eli Lilly's Zepbound and Mounjaro are now the company's growth engine—obesity drug revenue is outpacing traditional pharma—and Novo Nordisk just reported earnings that make it look like they got left at the gas station. The gap between Lilly's obesity franchise and traditional pharma isn't a gap anymore; it's a chasm. Novo's portfolio is in full reckoning mode. Novo cut guidance, Lilly raised it. That's the sector realignment happening in real time.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:01:18.038
<v Alex>The S&P 500 gained point two percent to close at 7739, Nasdaq up point three one percent at 26445. Small caps got nothing—Russell 2000 down point one one percent. VIX dropped to 15.79, so nobody's nervous. But zoom in and the story inverts: Eli Lilly up two point zero five percent to 1193 per share, Broadcom up one point three eight percent, Microsoft up one point five nine. Meanwhile, Salesforce dropped five point zero one percent, Accenture down three point four eight, Adobe down three point one. Enterprise software is in freefall and nobody at the index level cares because the mega-cap pharmaceutical and chip trades are so fat they're drowning out the noise.

00:01:18.038 --> 00:01:27.040
<v Jamie>So we've got this weird surface calm masking actual carnage in the B2B stack. Salesforce down five percent in one day—that's not a correction, that's a statement.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:45.044
<v Maya>The enterprise software sector is basically watching Lilly and Broadcom party while they're stuck in the corner doing their quarterly reviews. Salesforce, Accenture, Adobe all getting their lunch money taken, and the broader market just shrugs because the top ten stocks are carrying the whole thing on their backs like they're running a startup gym.
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/market-trend/stock-market-today/dow-jones-sp500-nasdaq-ai-stocks-sandisk-western-digital/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

00:01:45.044 --> 00:02:04.334
<v Alex>Gold up one point nine five percent to 4328, crude up one point two one percent at 76.13, ten-year Treasury jumped thirty-nine basis points to 4.63. The bond market's pricing in something—either inflation expectations are rising or the Fed narrative is shifting. Bitcoin down point two seven percent, so crypto's not catching a bid on the risk-off move either.

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:15.586
<v Jamie>Let's look under the hood here. Eight point five percent same-store sales growth—that's Burger King's number in the U.S. For context, that's not a bump. That's a full remodel. Where did that come from?

00:02:15.586 --> 00:02:31.339
<v Maya>Menu simplification and the chicken sandwich wars. Burger King went back to basics—stopped chasing every trend and started competing on the thing they're actually good at making. And it worked because fast food spent the last five years trying to become everything at once. Burger King said no.

00:02:31.339 --> 00:02:49.986
<v Alex>Here's the bear case though: franchisees are making money on eight point five percent comps because labor costs are sticky and they haven't repriced the menu to match inflation yet. That window closes. Once wage pressure hits hard or they have to discount again to defend share, those margins compress and you're back to the same cycle.

00:02:49.986 --> 00:03:07.025
<v Jamie>So it's not that Burger King figured something out—it's that they stopped bleeding and the market's rewarding the relief. Fair point. But here's what I want to know: are their peers panicking? Because if McDonald's or Wendy's see this and realize they have to move faster, that's when the real test happens.

00:03:07.025 --> 00:03:31.137
<v Maya>McDonald's has already been optimizing—they've got the scale and the tech. Wendy's is the one watching Burger King's playbook and thinking, 'Wait, we could have just... made better food?' But the second all three of them are fighting for the same customer on value, margins get ugly fast. Burger King got one quarter of sunshine. The question is whether they can keep the lights on when the whole sector's competing on the same field.

00:03:31.137 --> 00:03:38.210
<v Alex>Eli Lilly's obesity franchise is now generating more revenue than Novo Nordisk's entire pharma business. The structural gap isn't closing—it's calcifying.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:42.390
<v Jamie>So Novo's not just losing the race, they're losing the sport itself.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:54.928
<v Maya>Novo spent two decades building a traditional pharma moat and it turned out the moat was filled with the wrong water. Meanwhile Lilly showed up with a different pond entirely and now everyone's staring at the wrong horizon.

00:03:54.928 --> 00:04:05.216
<v Maya>SpaceX lockup just expired and the stock's tanking because the market's realizing Elon timed the IPO window perfectly—just before the hype collapsed. Insiders are selling into a narrative that's already cooling.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/06/spacex-faces-test-as-shares-unlock-allowing-early-investors-cash-out.html

00:04:05.216 --> 00:04:14.861
<v Alex>Peak IPO theater. You go public when the story's hottest, not when the fundamentals are strongest. The stock's doing what it always does—repricing to reality once the lockup lifts.

00:04:14.861 --> 00:04:23.541
<v Jamie>Jobless claims hit their lowest level since 1969. We're literally back to the moon-landing labor market—and that means wage pressure and inflation are not theoretical anymore.

00:04:23.541 --> 00:04:35.115
<v Alex>Layoffs have stopped because companies can't find people to fire without losing revenue. That's not a strong economy signal—that's a labor shortage signal. Wages go up, margins compress, and the Fed's disinflation story gets messier.

00:04:35.115 --> 00:04:47.332
<v Maya>SanDisk and Western Digital both tanked on earnings while Nvidia and the GPU crowd rallied. The market's saying storage is a commodity now but compute is still scarce. That's a brutal flip for the chip pecking order.

00:04:47.332 --> 00:04:55.369
<v Jamie>The hierarchy just inverted in one trading session. Storage used to be the choke point. Now it's invisible—everyone wants the thing that actually thinks.

00:04:55.369 --> 00:05:10.801
<v Maya>The Atlantic just published 'Fed Up With Romance' and it's a whole thing—young people are exiting the dating market because apps have turned finding a partner into a job interview where nobody gets hired. The piece is basically a four-thousand-word way of saying the effort-to-disappointment ratio broke.
  ↳ source: https://www.theatlantic.com/podcasts/2026/08/the-death-of-romance/688193/?utm_source=feed

00:05:10.801 --> 00:05:25.589
<v Jamie>So is this a real trend or is The Atlantic just discovering that rejection at scale feels worse than rejection in person? Because swiping through two hundred profiles and matching with none of them is a different kind of lonely than it used to be.

00:05:25.589 --> 00:05:48.094
<v Maya>Both. The data says single rates are up, marriage rates are down, and people are spending less time dating. But The Atlantic's angle is that the apps promised to solve the problem—infinite choice, algorithmic matching, no friction—and instead they just made the problem quantifiable. You can see exactly how many times you got passed over. It's like we automated rejection and then charged people a subscription to experience it.

00:05:48.094 --> 00:06:02.561
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow: we've got earnings rolling in on Wednesday. ACI Worldwide, Accelis Technologies, a couple others hitting at once. But here's what's actually worth watching—we're in the middle of this enterprise software bloodbath and nobody knows if it bottoms or keeps falling.

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:22.172
<v Alex>ACI Worldwide processes payments at scale—they report Wednesday. If their transaction volumes are soft or their guidance suggests clients are pulling back on spend, that's a real-time read on whether B2B budgets are actually freezing or if Salesforce and Adobe just had a bad quarter. The company sits between the banks and the merchants, so they see the actual flow.

00:06:22.172 --> 00:06:38.890
<v Maya>Meanwhile Accelis is semiconductor equipment—August sixth same day. So we get to watch the chip supply chain and the payment infrastructure report on the same morning. If Accelis guides down because fab orders are slowing, that tells you whether the GPU boom is actually sustaining or if it's already cannibalizing itself.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:44.677
<v Maya>Burger King's got one sunrise before the sector remembers it's still a restaurant business competing on price.

00:06:44.677 --> 00:06:49.178
<v Alex>And when the sun sets, eight point five percent evaporates into a footnote.

00:06:49.178 --> 00:06:53.679
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
